/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Call the source inductance Ls, in henries.
   - Integrating vs = Ls dis/dt over the overlap gives Vm[cos α − cos(α + μ)] = 2ωLs Id.
   - ω = 2πf in rad/s; Ls is the total AC-side inductance in H; Id is the DC current in A; Vm is the supply peak in V; ΔVd is the average DC voltage lost, in V.
   - Ls is the inductance per phase in H and VL the line RMS voltage in V.
   - Vd0 is the no-load output at α = 0: 2Vm/π (single-phase) or 3√2 VL/π (three-phase).
   - ΔVd/Id = 2ωLs/π or 3ωLs/π behaves like a resistance, the equivalent commutating resistance Rc.
   - During each overlap the supply terminals are shorted through the thyristors, so the voltage seen by other loads has notches, p of them per cycle.
   - In inverter mode the overlap eats into the time the outgoing thyristor needs to recover, which sets the largest usable firing angle.
*/
