/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A thyristor bridge cannot reverse its current, so the only way to reverse power P = Vdc Id is to reverse Vdc.
   - In steady state the averages obey Vdc = E + Id R, with E now negative and |E| > |Vdc|, so Id = (Vdc − E)/R > 0.
   - When the incoming thyristor is fired at α, the outgoing one must hand over its current and then block.
   - γ is the extinction angle in degrees; tq is the thyristor's circuit turn-off time in s; ω = 2πf in rad/s.
   - If γ is too short, the outgoing thyristor has not recovered when its voltage turns positive, so it conducts again.
   - Without overlap the limit is simply α_max = 180° − γ0.
   - Vd0 = 3√2 VL/π and ΔVd = 3ωLs Id/π.
   - So the heavier the current, the larger μ and the smaller α_max, and the less inverse voltage the converter can offer.
*/
