/* book exam traps: Saying the current reverses in inversion. It cannot; the voltage reverses. | Claiming a half-controlled bridge can invert. Its diodes clamp the output at zero. | Writing the limit as α ≤ 180°. It is α + μ ≤ 180° − γ0, and μ depends on the current. | Taking γ0 equal to ωtq. A margin above the turn-off time is always added. | Forgetting the source: an RL load alone cannot keep a converter inverting in steady state, because its average voltage is Id R > 0. */
